      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #25): (1↓V)←B on a scalar V
      ⍝ used to be SYNTAX ERROR ("mal-formed selective specification")
      ⍝ instead of the empty-selection no-op it was before r2127 --
      ⍝ identical in spirit to the still-working vector over-drop
      ⍝ (5↓V)←9. Root cause: the scalar-drop branch of Bif_F12_DROP::
      ⍝ eval_AB() copied B's own LvalCell into Z's prototype, then (for
      ⍝ the empty, i.e. fully-dropped, case) called to_type(false),
      ⍝ which rewrites that cell into a plain type-appropriate cell --
      ⍝ but Z was still separately marked set_left_value(), so
      ⍝ check_lval_consistency() (now called before assign_cellrefs()'s
      ⍝ dest_count==0 early return, Bugs30 #29) correctly rejected the
      ⍝ contradiction. Fixed by using an invalid/placeholder LvalCell(0,0)
      ⍝ prototype instead of to_type(false) whenever B is itself a left
      ⍝ value, matching the no-op pattern used elsewhere for "nothing
      ⍝ real to select".
      ⍝
      V←5
      (1↓V)←9
      V
