      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs28 #67, 2026-08-11): A↓[X]B with an
      ⍝ empty X used to return B unchanged without even looking at A --
      ⍝ its "no axes" shortcut ran before A was type- or length-checked
      ⍝ against X, unlike A↑[X]B (Bif_F12_TAKE::eval_AXB), which checks
      ⍝ first. So e.g. a wrongly-shaped or wrongly-typed A silently did
      ⍝ nothing instead of failing with LENGTH ERROR like ↑ does.
      ⍝
      M←2 3⍴⍳6
      2↓[⍬]M
LENGTH ERROR+
      2↓[⍬]M
      ^^

      'abc'↓[⍬]M
LENGTH ERROR+
      'abc'↓[⍬]M
      ^    ^

      ⍝ control: ↑ already got this right
      ⍝
      2↑[⍬]M
LENGTH ERROR+
      2↑[⍬]M
      ^^

      )SIC

      ⍝ control: matching (empty) A and X still work
      ⍝
      ⍬↓[⍬]M
1 2 3
4 5 6

      )ERASE M
